  to make it correct, which isn't in scope today.
  [3mComplexity:[0m moderate, same as A, plus expiry/logout work to be safe.

  [1mC. Server-owned session, [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m cookie, client caches nothing durable[0m
  The server sets an [38;5;153mHttpOnly[39m session cookie on login; JS cannot read it. The client module holds [38;5;153muserId[39m in memory only
  and re-hydrates by calling a [38;5;153m/me[39m endpoint on page load.
  [3mTrade-offs:[0m the genuinely correct model for real authentication — the browser can't lie about identity and XSS can't
  exfiltrate the session. But it requires a backend that does not exist here, and it makes every page load depend on a
  network round-trip. It also makes the client module a cache rather than a source of truth, which is a different shape
  from A/B.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high, and currently unbuildable in this repo.

  [1mMy recommendation: A[0m, with the server contract written into the spec so C stays reachable. Reasoning: [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m
  matches the actual lifetime of a login session without inventing an expiry policy we haven't discussed, and it's the
  only option of the three that's fully buildable and testable against the repo as it stands. B's extra durability isn't
  free — it's a security and correctness commitment (logout, expiry, shared machines) that you'd be taking on
  implicitly. C is where this should land once there's a real backend, and specifying the contract now is what keeps
  that migration from being a rewrite.

  One caveat I want on the record regardless of choice: [1m[38;5;153muserId[39m in client storage must never be treated as authorization.
[0m  It says who the client claims to be, not who they've proven to be. Any server endpoint must re-derive identity from
  the session cookie or token, never from a [38;5;153muserId[39m the client sends. I'll write that into the spec as a constraint.
